  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $x>0$. Prove that $x+\\frac1x\\ge2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x+\\frac1x\\ge2$.\n\nSubtracting $2$ gives $\\frac{(x-1)^2}{x}\\ge0$, since the numerator is non-negative and $x>0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac1x-2&=\\frac{x^2+1-2x}{x}\\\\x+\\frac1x-2&=\\frac{(x-1)^2}{x}\\\\x+\\frac1x-2&\\ge0\\end{align*}$$\nThe numerator is non-negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore $x+\\frac1x\\ge2$, with equality when $x=1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
